      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To express the repeating decimal \\(0.5\\overline{10}\\) as a common fraction, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Let \\( x = 0.5\\overline{10} \\).\n2. To eliminate the repeating part, multiply \\( x \\) by 100 (since the repeating part \"10\" has two digits):\n   \\[\n   100x = 51.\\overline{10}\n   \\]\n3. Subtract the original \\( x = 0.5\\overline{10} \\) from \\( 100x \\):\n   \\[\n   100x - x = 51.\\overline{10} - 0.5\\overline{10}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the left side, we get:\n   \\[\n   99x = 50.6\n   \\]\n4. Solve for \\( x \\) by dividing both sides by 99:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{50.6}{99}\n   \\]\n5. Convert the decimal in the numerator to a fraction:\n   \\[\n   50.6 = 50 + 0.6 = 50 + \\frac{6}{10} = 50 + \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{250}{5} + \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{253}{5}\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{253}{5} \\div 99 = \\frac{253}{5 \\cdot 99} = \\frac{253}{495}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the common fraction representation of \\(0.5\\overline{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{253}{495}}\\).",
